  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n- Brief Period of Rain this Morning\n\nA shortwave trough is moving through this morning with plenty of\npositive vorticity advection and 700 mb frontogenesis. These\nshowers are elevated as seen in observations with cloud bases\naround 8 to 10 kft. As radar trends continue it looks like most\nwill see light rain before the 850 mb front clears around 9am.\n\nDry conditions are expected through the remainder of the forecast,\nbut Tuesday night into Wednesday will need to be monitored. A\ndecent low level jet, warm air advection ahead of the shortwave\ntrough and front will be present. The question is will there be\nenough moisture. Currently better chances are north of the area,\nbut the ENS members highlight light rain across the forecast area.\nIf this trend continues, chances for precipitation will need to be\nadded.\n\n- Areas of Frost Possible Tonight and Monday Night\n\nHigh pressure builds in tonight and continues into Monday. 925 mb\ntemperatures will fall into the 6 to 8 C range. The inversion that\ndevelops isn't overly strong so expecting inland areas along and\nnorth of U-10 to have the best chance for lows dropping into the\nmid 30s with isolated colder spots. However confidence is not high\nat this time as there has been a slight warming trend in the data.\nIn coordination with Gaylord held off on issuing a Frost Advisory\nat this time due to the uncertainty.\n\nMonday night will have a better chance for more widespread frost\nas low temperatures drop into the mid to upper 30s across areas\nlargely east of US-131 and north of I-96. The best chances (50 \npercent) for lows in the low to mid 30s will be inland areas along\nand north of US-10.",
